PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L11U4.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":11,"cefr":"C1","unit":4,"theme":"The Power of Persuasion","essential_question":"What makes an argument convincing, and how can we resist manipulation?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1","title":"I Think So, If Not, Others Don't: Substitution and Ellipsis","objectives":["Students will be able to avoid repetition by substituting so, not, do so, one and possessive pronouns.","Students will be able to use ellipsis after auxiliaries and to without changing the meaning.","I can answer \"Is the claim true?\" with \"I don't think so\" or \"I'm afraid not\".","I can write \"Some advertisers use honest arguments; others don't.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تجنب التكرار باستبدال so وnot وdo so وone والضمائر الملكية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الحذف بعد الأفعال المساعدة وto دون تغيير المعنى.","أستطيع أن أجيب عن Is the claim true? بـ I don't think so أو I'm afraid not.","أستطيع أن أكتب Some advertisers use honest arguments; others don't."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"rhetoric","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛtɚɪk/","definition":"the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people","arabic":"البلاغة / فن الخطابة","example":"Students of rhetoric study famous speeches.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W01"},{"word":"anecdote","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænəkˌdoʊt/","definition":"a short, interesting story about a real event or person","arabic":"حكاية قصيرة / طرفة","example":"The speaker began with a funny anecdote.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W02"},{"word":"appeal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpil/","definition":"an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust","arabic":"مناشدة / استمالة","example":"The advertisement makes an emotional appeal.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W03"},{"word":"counterargument","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊntɚˌɑrɡjəmənt/","definition":"an argument that opposes another argument","arabic":"حجة مضادة","example":"A good essay answers the main counterargument.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Substitution replaces words to avoid repetition. so / not replace a whole clause after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, guess, be afraid, appear, seem: Is logic enough? Many people think so. Will it fail? I hope not. I'm afraid not. Note: I don't think so is more natural than I think not (formal). if so = if this is true; if not = if this is not true.\ndo so replaces a verb phrase in formal English: The committee promised to review the policy, and it did so last week. one / ones replace a countable noun: This argument is stronger than the last one. I prefer the shorter speeches to the long ones. Don't use one for uncountable nouns: (not: a good one advice) → some good advice. Possessive pronouns: Advertisers sell their products; influencers sell theirs.\nEllipsis leaves words out after an auxiliary or modal: Some rely on honest arguments; others don't. I haven't read the book, but Sara has. Can we recognize fallacies? Experts believe we can. so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject: She can speak French, and so can I. He didn't agree, and neither did I. After to: You don't have to come if you don't want to. I'd love to.\nthink / hope / be afraid + so / not (I think so, I hope not)\nif so / if not • do so (formal verb phrase)\none / ones (countable) • theirs / ours / mine\nellipsis after auxiliaries and modals • so / neither + auxiliary + subject • want to / have to","examples":["Is logic alone enough? Many people think so.","Will the advertisement persuade you? Hopefully not.","Read the claim. If so, check the source; if not, ignore it.","The council promised to act, and it did so immediately.","This argument is stronger than the last one.","Some rely on honest arguments; others don't.","I haven't seen the debate, but Lina has.","She can speak three languages, and so can her brother."],"common_errors":["✗ Is it true? I don't think it. → ✓ Is it true? I don't think so. (Substitute with so.)","✗ Will it rain? I hope not to. → ✓ Will it rain? I hope not. (hope not.)","✗ She gave me a good one advice. → ✓ She gave me some good advice. (advice is uncountable – no one.)","✗ Some speakers prepare carefully; others don't prepare carefully. → ✓ Some speakers prepare carefully; others don't. (Ellipsis avoids repetition.)"],"arabic":"الاستبدال يمنع التكرار: so وnot تحلّان محل جملة كاملة بعد think وhope وbe afraid: I think so / I hope not / I'm afraid not، وI don't think so أكثر شيوعًا من I think not.\nif so = إن كان كذلك، وif not = وإلا، وdo so تحل محل فعل وما بعده في الأسلوب الرسمي.\none / ones تحل محل الاسم المعدود فقط، والضمائر الملكية مثل theirs تحل محل الاسم مع صاحبه.\nالحذف بعد الأفعال المساعدة: Some do; others don't. وso / neither + فعل مساعد + فاعل، وبعد to: I'd love to."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Think So, If Not, Others Don't: Substitution and Ellipsis”. Let’s learn substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U4-LS1 | I Think So, If Not, Others Don't: Substitution and Ellipsis","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “The Art of Persuasion”: Why do some speakers move their audiences while others do not? More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle offered an answer, and many teachers of rhetoric still consider it the most useful one. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Why do some speakers move their audiences while others do not? More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle offered an answer, and many teachers of rhetoric still consider it the most useful one.","visual":"Image GV-L11-U4-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Substitution replaces words to avoid repetition. so / not replace a whole clause after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, guess, be afraid, appear, seem: Is logic enough? Many people think so. Will it fail? I hope not. I'm afraid not. Note: I don't think so is more natural than I think not (formal). if so = if this is true; if not = if this is not true. do so replaces a verb phrase in formal English: The committee promised to review the policy, and it did so last week. one / ones replace a countable noun: This argument is stronger than the last one. I prefer the shorter speeches to the long ones. Don't use one for uncountable nouns: (not: a good one advice) → some good advice. Possessive pronouns: Advertisers sell their products; influencers sell theirs.","on_screen":"think / hope / be afraid + so / not (I think so, I hope not) • if so / if not • do so (formal verb phrase) • one / ones (countable) • theirs / ours / mine","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Is logic alone enough? Many people think so. Will the advertisement persuade you? Hopefully not. Read the claim. If so, check the source; if not, ignore it.","on_screen":"Is logic alone enough? Many people think so. / Will the advertisement persuade you? Hopefully not. / Read the claim. If so, check the source; if not, ignore it.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Is it true? I don't think it.” That’s not right. We say: “Is it true? I don't think so.” Substitute with so.","on_screen":"✗ Is it true? I don't think it.   ✓ Is it true? I don't think so.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Is it true?\" – \"I don't think ___.\" … Think! … *so*. Great job! Now you can say: I can answer \"Is the claim true?\" with \"I don't think so\" or \"I'm afraid not\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can answer \"Is the claim true?\" with \"I don't think so\" or \"I'm afraid not\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الاستبدال يمنع التكرار: so وnot تحلّان محل جملة كاملة بعد think وhope وbe afraid: I think so / I hope not / I'm afraid not، وI don't think so أكثر شيوعًا من I think not.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Is it true? I don't think it. — الصحيح: Is it true? I don't think so.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أجيب عن Is the claim true? بـ I don't think so أو I'm afraid not."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Art of Persuasion","body":"Why do some speakers move their audiences while others do not? More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle offered an answer, and many teachers of rhetoric still consider it the most useful one. He argued that persuasion depends on three appeals: ethos, pathos and logos.\nEthos is the speaker's credibility. If listeners trust a speaker, they are likely to accept her arguments; if not, even strong evidence may be ignored. Pathos appeals to emotions. A moving anecdote can sway an audience more effectively than a page of statistics, although a careful listener should ask whether the story is typical. Logos is logic: clear reasons supported by reliable evidence.\nIs logic alone enough to persuade? Many people think so. Aristotle did not. He believed that the most compelling speakers combine all three appeals, and modern studies of successful speeches appear to confirm that they do so.\nThe Arab world has its own rich tradition of eloquence. In the ninth century, the scholar Al-Jahiz wrote a famous book on clear and effective expression, and poets and preachers have long been admired for theirs.\nToday, persuasion is everywhere. Advertisers want us to buy their products, and influencers want us to buy theirs. Some rely on honest arguments; others don't. Logical fallacies, such as attacking a person instead of the argument, are common online. Can we learn to recognize them? Experts believe we can.\nStudying rhetoric, then, is not only for politicians and lawyers. Anyone who wants to argue fairly, and to notice when others are not doing so, will benefit. Will the next advertisement you see persuade you? Hopefully not, unless its claims are supported by evidence.","word_count":277,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"\"Is logic alone enough to persuade?\" \"Many people think ___.\"","options":["yes","so","it","that"],"answer":"so","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"\"Will the advertisement persuade you?\" \"I hope ___.\"","options":["not so","no","not","don't"],"answer":"not","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"Some advertisers rely on honest arguments; others ___.","options":["don't","aren't","don't rely","not"],"answer":"don't","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"This argument is much stronger than the last ___.","options":["one","it","that one argument","ones"],"answer":"one","explanation":"one / ones; possessive pronouns.","explanation_ar":"one / ones."},{"q":"The committee promised to review the policy, and it ___ the following week.","options":["so did","made so","did so","did it so"],"answer":"did so","explanation":"do so (formal).","explanation_ar":"do so."},{"q":"Readers should first decide whether a website is reliable; ______, they can use its information with more confidence.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["so not","not so","if not","if so"],"answer":"if so","explanation":"if so / if not.","explanation_ar":"if so / if not."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Replace the words in brackets to avoid repetition. Word bank: has, so, to","items":[{"q":"\"Is the source reliable?\" \"I believe ______ (that the source is reliable).\"","options":[],"answer":"so","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"Faisal hasn't read the article, but Lina ______ (has read the article).","options":[],"answer":"has","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"You don't have to speak at the debate if you don't want ______ (to speak at the debate).","options":[],"answer":"to","explanation":"so / neither + auxiliary + subject; to-ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"so do I."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"\"Is the claim true?\" \"I don't think it.\"","options":[],"answer":"\"Is the claim true?\" \"I don't think so.\"","explanation":"it → so. so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: it → so. so / not."},{"q":"She gave me a good one advice about public speaking.","options":[],"answer":"She gave me some good advice about public speaking.","explanation":"a good one advice → some good advice. one / ones; possessive pronouns.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: a good one advice → some good advice. one / ones."},{"q":"He didn't believe the advertisement, and neither I did.","options":[],"answer":"He didn't believe the advertisement, and neither did I.","explanation":"neither I did → neither did I. so / neither + auxiliary + subject; to-ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: neither I did → neither did I. so do I."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Answer without repeating.","items":[{"q":"\"Will online debates become more polite?\" Give a short answer with \"so\" or \"not\".","options":[],"answer":"I'm afraid not.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"if so\".","options":[],"answer":"Check whether the article has a named author; if so, look up the author's qualifications.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"do so\".","options":[],"answer":"The speaker promised to answer every question, and she did so patiently.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"one\" or \"ones\".","options":[],"answer":"I prefer short, clear speeches to long ones.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with ellipsis after a modal.","options":[],"answer":"Some people can speak without notes; others can't.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A short dialogue.","items":[{"q":"Write a 2-line dialogue with \"I hope so\" or \"I'm afraid not\".","options":[],"answer":"\"Did our team win the debate?\" \"I'm afraid not.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences comparing two speakers with ellipsis.","options":[],"answer":"Sara used statistics, but Lina didn't. Lina told a story, and so did Yusuf.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"theirs\" or \"ours\".","options":[],"answer":"Their argument was emotional; ours was based on evidence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Four Corners Grammar","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a sentence frame with a cue word and four possible answers (A–D).","Students walk to the corner with the correct answer.","One student in each corner explains the choice in one sentence.","Correct corner scores a point; continue with the next cue."],"data":[{"label":"I think so"},{"label":"I'm afraid not"},{"label":"if so"},{"label":"if not"},{"label":"did so"},{"label":"a better one"},{"label":"others don't"},{"label":"so can I"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"\"Has the speaker finished?\" \"I think ___.\"","options":["it","that","so","yes"],"answer":"so","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"\"Is the website trustworthy?\" \"I'm afraid ___.\"","options":["so not","don't","not","no"],"answer":"not","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"Is the statistic accurate? ___, we can use it in our essay.","options":["Not if","So if","If not","If so"],"answer":"If so","explanation":"if so / if not.","explanation_ar":"if so / if not."},{"q":"Students who want to join the debate team should ___ before Friday.","options":["so do","make so","do it so","do so"],"answer":"do so","explanation":"do so (formal).","explanation_ar":"do so."},{"q":"I like this speech, but I prefer the first ___.","options":["ones","one","it","this"],"answer":"one","explanation":"one / ones; possessive pronouns.","explanation_ar":"one / ones."},{"q":"","options":["Sara has watched the debate, and so has Noura.","Sara has watched the debate, and Noura so has.","Sara has watched the debate, and so does Noura.","Sara has watched the debate, and so Noura has."],"answer":"Sara has watched the debate, and so has Noura.","explanation":"so / neither + auxiliary + subject; to-ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"so do I."},{"q":"","options":["Some speakers use notes; others don't.","Some speakers use notes; others aren't.","Some speakers use notes; others don't use.","Some speakers use notes; others not."],"answer":"Some speakers use notes; others don't.","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"Many advertisements claim that their products are \"scientifically proven,\" but few of them can show evidence that they ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["have","can be","are","do"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"Persuasive writers should acknowledge the strongest counterargument and, if possible, answer it; many effective writers ______ in the second paragraph of their essays.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["do it so","do so","so do","does so"],"answer":"do so","explanation":"do so (formal).","explanation_ar":"do so."},{"q":"\"Is logic alone enough?\" \"Aristotle did not think so.\" What does \"so\" replace?","options":["persuasion","that logic alone is enough","Aristotle","logic"],"answer":"that logic alone is enough","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A debate hall: one student at a podium, another raising a hand; speech bubbles \"I don't think so\", \"If so, why…?\", \"Some do; others don't\"; a large screen showing three circles labeled Ethos, Pathos, Logos. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A debate hall: one student at a podium, another raising a hand; speech bubbles \"I don't think so\", \"If so, why…?\", \"Some do; others don't\"; a large screen showing three circles labeled Ethos, Pathos, Logos"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.), with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so)"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows rhetoric (the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing rhetoric: the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows anecdote (a short, interesting story about a real event or person); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing anecdote: a short, interesting story about a real event or person"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows appeal (an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing appeal: an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows counterargument (an argument that opposes another argument); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing counterargument: an argument that opposes another argument"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Four Corners Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Four Corners Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W01","script":"rhetoric. rhetoric. Students of rhetoric study famous speeches.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W02","script":"anecdote. anecdote. The speaker began with a funny anecdote.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W03","script":"appeal. appeal. The advertisement makes an emotional appeal.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-W04","script":"counterargument. counterargument. A good essay answers the main counterargument.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-T1","script":"Why do some speakers move their audiences while others do not? More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle offered an answer, and many teachers of rhetoric still consider it the most useful one. He argued that persuasion depends on three appeals: ethos, pathos and logos. Ethos is the speaker's credibility. If listeners trust a speaker, they are likely to accept her arguments; if not, even strong evidence may be ignored. Pathos appeals to emotions. A moving anecdote can sway an audience more effectively than a page of statistics, although a careful listener should ask whether the story is typical. Logos is logic: clear reasons supported by reliable evidence. Is logic alone enough to persuade? Many people think so. Aristotle did not. He believed that the most compelling speakers combine all three appeals, and modern studies of successful speeches appear to confirm that they do so. The Arab world has its own rich tradition of eloquence. In the ninth century, the scholar Al-Jahiz wrote a famous book on clear and effective expression, and poets and preachers have long been admired for theirs. Today, persuasion is everywhere. Advertisers want us to buy their products, and influencers want us to buy theirs. Some rely on honest arguments; others don't. Logical fallacies, such as attacking a person instead of the argument, are common online. Can we learn to recognize them? Experts believe we can. Studying rhetoric, then, is not only for politicians and lawyers. Anyone who wants to argue fairly, and to notice when others are not doing so, will benefit. Will the next advertisement you see persuade you? Hopefully not, unless its claims are supported by evidence.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Think So, If Not, Others Don't: Substitution and Ellipsis”. Let’s learn substitution with so and not after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, be afraid, appear, seem (I think so / I hope not / I don't think so); if so / if not; do so (formal); one / ones for countable nouns; possessive pronouns (theirs, ours); the same; ellipsis after auxiliaries (Some do; others don't. I haven't, but Sara has.); so / neither / nor + auxiliary + subject; ellipsis of the verb after to (I'd like to.) together. Listen to the start of “The Art of Persuasion”: Why do some speakers move their audiences while others do not? More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle offered an answer, and many teachers of rhetoric still consider it the most useful one. Look at the green words. Substitution replaces words to avoid repetition. so / not replace a whole clause after think, hope, expect, suppose, believe, guess, be afraid, appear, seem: Is logic enough? Many people think so. Will it fail? I hope not. I'm afraid not. Note: I don't think so is more natural than I think not (formal). if so = if this is true; if not = if this is not true. do so replaces a verb phrase in formal English: The committee promised to review the policy, and it did so last week. one / ones replace a countable noun: This argument is stronger than the last one. I prefer the shorter speeches to the long ones. Don't use one for uncountable nouns: (not: a good one advice) → some good advice. Possessive pronouns: Advertisers sell their products; influencers sell theirs. Here are some examples. Is logic alone enough? Many people think so. Will the advertisement persuade you? Hopefully not. Read the claim. If so, check the source; if not, ignore it. Be careful! Some learners say: “Is it true? I don't think it.” That’s not right. We say: “Is it true? I don't think so.” Substitute with so. Quick check: \"Is it true?\" – \"I don't think ___.\" … Think! … *so*. Great job! Now you can say: I can answer \"Is the claim true?\" with \"I don't think so\" or \"I'm afraid not\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2","title":"In Other Words, What Is More, the Latter: Discourse Markers and Cohesion","objectives":["Students will be able to choose discourse markers that match the function of the sentence.","Students will be able to make texts cohesive with summary nouns, the former / the latter and synonyms.","I can use \"in other words\" to explain an idea again more simply.","I can write \"Sara and Lina spoke; the former used statistics, while the latter told a story.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار أدوات الخطاب التي تناسب وظيفة الجملة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من جعل النصوص متماسكة بأسماء التلخيص وthe former / the latter والمرادفات.","أستطيع أن أستخدم in other words لأشرح الفكرة مرة أخرى بشكل أبسط.","أستطيع أن أكتب Sara and Lina spoke; the former used statistics, while the latter told a story."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"rebuttal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈbʌtəl/","definition":"a response that shows an argument is wrong","arabic":"تفنيد / رد","example":"Her rebuttal was clear and well supported.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W01"},{"word":"fallacy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfæləsi/","definition":"a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning","arabic":"مغالطة","example":"Attacking the speaker instead of the argument is a common fallacy.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W02"},{"word":"emotive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ɪˈmoʊtɪv/","definition":"causing strong feelings","arabic":"مثير للعواطف","example":"The article uses emotive language to shock readers.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W03"},{"word":"eloquent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛləkwənt/","definition":"able to speak or write clearly and beautifully","arabic":"فصيح / بليغ","example":"He gave an eloquent speech at graduation.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Discourse markers tell the reader how ideas connect. Organizing: to begin with, firstly, secondly, finally, all in all, to sum up. Adding: what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact. Reformulating (saying it again more clearly): in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely. Exemplifying: for instance, a case in point is…\nContrasting and conceding: on the one hand… on the other hand; while / whereas (inside one sentence); admittedly (accepting a point); after all (giving a reason that the listener should remember: Don't blame him; after all, he's only fourteen.). Spoken markers: well (thinking), anyway (returning to the main topic), mind you (adding a small contrast). Don't use spoken markers in formal essays.\nCohesion means the text holds together. Use reference: this / these / such + summary noun (This argument…, These claims…, Such tactics…) instead of a bare this. Use the former (the first of two) and the latter (the second of two). Use lexical cohesion: synonyms and word families (persuade → persuasion → persuasive) instead of repeating the same word.\nreformulate: in other words • that is to say • to put it another way • namely\nadd: what is more • indeed • as a matter of fact • besides\nconcede / reason: admittedly • after all • on the one hand… on the other hand\ncohesion: this + summary noun • the former / the latter • synonyms","examples":["To begin with, the claim has no evidence.","The ad uses emotive language; in other words, it tries to frighten us.","The speech was long; what is more, it was repetitive.","Three factors matter, namely trust, emotion and logic.","Admittedly, the speaker was nervous; even so, her points were strong.","Don't judge him too harshly; after all, it was his first debate.","Sara and Lina both spoke; the former used statistics, while the latter told a story.","Some sites spread false claims. These claims can mislead millions."],"common_errors":["✗ The speech was persuasive. Anyway, it had three main parts. (essay) → ✓ The speech was persuasive. To begin with, it had a clear structure. (No spoken markers in essays.)","✗ Advertisers use fear and humor. This works well. → ✓ Advertisers use fear and humor. These tactics work well. (Summary noun after this / these.)","✗ Sara and Lina spoke. The latter used statistics, the former told a story about her grandfather. → ✓ Sara and Lina spoke; the former (Sara) used statistics, while the latter (Lina) told a story. (Use former / latter in order.)","✗ In other words, here is another example. → ✓ For instance, here is another example. (Example → for instance.)"],"arabic":"أدوات الخطاب ترشد القارئ: للترتيب to begin with وfinally، وللإضافة what is more وindeed، ولإعادة الصياغة in other words وthat is to say وnamely، وللتمثيل for instance.\nللمقابلة والتنازل: on the one hand… on the other hand وadmittedly، وafter all تقدّم سببًا ينبغي تذكّره.\nأدوات الكلام مثل well وanyway وmind you لا تُستخدم في المقالات الرسمية.\nالتماسك: this / these + اسم ملخِّص (this argument)، وthe former للأول وthe latter للثاني، والمرادفات وعائلات الكلمات."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “In Other Words, What Is More, the Latter: Discourse Markers and Cohesion”. Let’s learn discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U4-LS2 | In Other Words, What Is More, the Latter: Discourse Markers and Cohesion","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: In other words, the advertisement is misleading. The argument is not convincing; what is more, it is unfair. What, then, is the solution?","on_screen":"In other words, the advertisement is misleading. | The argument is not convincing; what is more, it is unfair. | What, then, is the solution?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Discourse markers tell the reader how ideas connect. Organizing: to begin with, firstly, secondly, finally, all in all, to sum up. Adding: what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact. Reformulating (saying it again more clearly): in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely. Exemplifying: for instance, a case in point is… Contrasting and conceding: on the one hand… on the other hand; while / whereas (inside one sentence); admittedly (accepting a point); after all (giving a reason that the listener should remember: Don't blame him; after all, he's only fourteen.). Spoken markers: well (thinking), anyway (returning to the main topic), mind you (adding a small contrast). Don't use spoken markers in formal essays.","on_screen":"reformulate: in other words • that is to say • to put it another way • namely • add: what is more • indeed • as a matter of fact • besides • concede / reason: admittedly • after all • on the one hand… on the other hand","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. To begin with, the claim has no evidence. The ad uses emotive language; in other words, it tries to frighten us. The speech was long; what is more, it was repetitive.","on_screen":"To begin with, the claim has no evidence. / The ad uses emotive language; in other words, it tries to frighten us. / The speech was long; what is more, it was repetitive.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The speech was persuasive. Anyway, it had three main parts. (essay).” That’s not right. We say: “The speech was persuasive. To begin with, it had a clear structure.” No spoken markers in essays.","on_screen":"✗ The speech was persuasive. Anyway, it had three main parts. (essay)   ✓ The speech was persuasive. To begin with, it had a clear structure.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which marker means \"I'll say it again more simply\"? … Think! … *In other words*. Great job! Now you can say: I can use \"in other words\" to explain an idea again more simply.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can use \"in other words\" to explain an idea again more simply.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","أدوات الخطاب ترشد القارئ: للترتيب to begin with وfinally، وللإضافة what is more وindeed، ولإعادة الصياغة in other words وthat is to say وnamely، وللتمثيل for instance.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The speech was persuasive. Anyway, it had three main parts. (essay) — الصحيح: The speech was persuasive. To begin with, it had a clear structure.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أستخدم in other words لأشرح الفكرة مرة أخرى بشكل أبسط."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The advertisement uses frightening images; ___, it tries to scare us into buying.","options":["for instance","after all","in other words","to begin with"],"answer":"in other words","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."},{"q":"The speech was far too long; ___, it repeated the same point five times.","options":["in other words","after all","namely","what is more"],"answer":"what is more","explanation":"Adding and emphasizing.","explanation_ar":"الإضافة والتوكيد."},{"q":"Persuasion depends on three appeals, ___ ethos, pathos and logos.","options":["after all","for instance","namely","anyway"],"answer":"namely","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."},{"q":"Sara and Lina both spoke. ___ used statistics, while the latter told a moving story.","options":["The former","The latter","The first one","Former"],"answer":"The former","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."},{"q":"Don't be too hard on him; ___, it was his first debate.","options":["after all","in other words","namely","finally"],"answer":"after all","explanation":"Contrast, concession, after all.","explanation_ar":"المقابلة والتنازل."},{"q":"Many websites present opinions as if they were facts. ______ can mislead readers who do not check the sources.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["It","This practice","They","This"],"answer":"This practice","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with a discourse marker: namely, in other words, admittedly, what is more. Word bank: what is more, in other words, Admittedly","items":[{"q":"______, the speaker was nervous; even so, her arguments were strong.","options":[],"answer":"Admittedly","explanation":"Contrast, concession, after all.","explanation_ar":"المقابلة والتنازل."},{"q":"The claim had no evidence; ______, the source was anonymous.","options":[],"answer":"what is more","explanation":"Adding and emphasizing.","explanation_ar":"الإضافة والتوكيد."},{"q":"The article is biased; ______, it presents only one side.","options":[],"answer":"in other words","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Advertisers use fear and humor. This can be very effective.","options":[],"answer":"Advertisers use fear and humor. These tactics can be very effective.","explanation":"This → These tactics. Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: This → These tactics. الإحالة."},{"q":"Anyway, the essay has a clear structure. (formal essay)","options":[],"answer":"To begin with, the essay has a clear structure. (formal essay)","explanation":"Anyway → To begin with. Spoken vs written markers.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Anyway → To begin with. المنطوق والمكتوب."},{"q":"The essay has two weaknesses, for instance weak evidence and no counterargument.","options":[],"answer":"The essay has two weaknesses, namely weak evidence and no counterargument.","explanation":"for instance → namely. Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: for instance → namely. إعادة الصياغة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Connect your ideas.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"in other words\" explaining a difficult idea simply.","options":[],"answer":"The claim is unfalsifiable; in other words, no evidence could ever prove it wrong.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"the former… the latter…\".","options":[],"answer":"Facts and stories both persuade; the former convince the mind, while the latter move the heart.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"after all\".","options":[],"answer":"We should listen to young people's ideas; after all, they will inherit the future.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two sentences linked by \"This + summary noun\".","options":[],"answer":"Some influencers hide paid advertisements. This practice misleads their followers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"on the one hand… on the other hand\".","options":[],"answer":"On the one hand, social media spreads information quickly; on the other hand, it spreads rumors just as fast.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A cohesive paragraph.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 linked sentences about advertising with three different discourse markers.","options":[],"answer":"To begin with, many advertisements exaggerate. What is more, some hide important information. In other words, consumers must read carefully.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite to avoid repetition: \"Persuasion is important. Persuasion is used in ads. Persuasion can be dangerous.\"","options":[],"answer":"Persuasion is important. It is used in advertising, and its techniques can be dangerous.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"namely\".","options":[],"answer":"A strong argument needs two things, namely clear reasons and reliable evidence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Snakes & Ladders","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Roll and move. If you land on a cue square, make a sentence with the cue and discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families).","Correct at the bottom of a ladder: climb. Incorrect at the head of a snake: slide down.","Teammates are the judges and must explain any correction.","First player to reach square 30 wins."],"data":[{"cue":"Is the argument convincing? / think (so)"},{"cue":"Will it rain? / hope (not)"},{"cue":"if the claim is true / (if so)"},{"cue":"if the claim is false / (if not)"},{"cue":"promise to review / and / do (did so)"},{"cue":"a better argument / (one)"},{"cue":"some use honest arguments / others (don't)"},{"cue":"she can speak French / I (so can I)"},{"cue":"I haven't read it / Sara (has)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___, I'd like to thank the organizers for inviting me.","options":["Namely","In other words","To begin with","After all"],"answer":"To begin with","explanation":"Organizing markers.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الترتيب."},{"q":"The claim is false; ___, it is dangerous.","options":["namely","in other words","for instance","indeed"],"answer":"indeed","explanation":"Adding and emphasizing.","explanation_ar":"الإضافة والتوكيد."},{"q":"The speaker was very persuasive; ___, she changed many people's minds.","options":["in fact","anyway","to begin with","namely"],"answer":"in fact","explanation":"Adding and emphasizing.","explanation_ar":"الإضافة والتوكيد."},{"q":"Two qualities make a good debater, ___ confidence and good listening.","options":["anyway","namely","after all","in other words"],"answer":"namely","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."},{"q":"We have discussed ethos and logos; ___ is about trust, the latter about reasoning.","options":["the former","the latter","the first one","former"],"answer":"the former","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."},{"q":"","options":["Admittedly, the evidence is limited; in other words, the argument is convincing.","Admittedly, the evidence is limited; therefore, the argument is unconvincing as a result.","Admittedly, the evidence is limited; nevertheless, the argument is convincing.","Admittedly, the evidence is limited; namely, the argument is convincing."],"answer":"Admittedly, the evidence is limited; nevertheless, the argument is convincing.","explanation":"Contrast, concession, after all.","explanation_ar":"المقابلة والتنازل."},{"q":"In a formal essay, which marker should you avoid?","options":["Anyway,","In other words,","To sum up,","Moreover,"],"answer":"Anyway,","explanation":"Spoken vs written markers.","explanation_ar":"المنطوق والمكتوب."},{"q":"Some politicians answer difficult questions by changing the subject. ______ allow them to avoid giving clear answers without appearing to refuse.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["This one","Such tactics","Such","It"],"answer":"Such tactics","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."},{"q":"The advertisement never states directly that the drink improves performance; ______, it suggests this idea through images of famous athletes.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["what is more","instead","after all","namely"],"answer":"instead","explanation":"Contrast, concession, after all.","explanation_ar":"المقابلة والتنازل."},{"q":"What does \"to put it another way\" signal?","options":["The writer is adding a new point.","The writer is concluding.","The writer is giving a contrast.","The writer is restating an idea."],"answer":"The writer is restating an idea.","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"An essay on a desk with colored highlighting: green for \"To begin with\", blue for \"In other words\", orange for \"What is more\", purple for \"the former / the latter\"; arrows connecting \"this problem\" back to the sentence before it. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An essay on a desk with colored highlighting: green for \"To begin with\", blue for \"In other words\", orange for \"What is more\", purple for \"the former / the latter\"; arrows connecting \"this problem\" back to the sentence before it"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “The Power of Persuasion”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is)"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows rebuttal (a response that shows an argument is wrong); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing rebuttal: a response that shows an argument is wrong"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows fallacy (a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing fallacy: a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows emotive (causing strong feelings); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing emotive: causing strong feelings"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows eloquent (able to speak or write clearly and beautifully); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing eloquent: able to speak or write clearly and beautifully"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Snakes & Ladders”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Snakes & Ladders"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W01","script":"rebuttal. rebuttal. Her rebuttal was clear and well supported.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W02","script":"fallacy. fallacy. Attacking the speaker instead of the argument is a common fallacy.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W03","script":"emotive. emotive. The article uses emotive language to shock readers.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-W04","script":"eloquent. eloquent. He gave an eloquent speech at graduation.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “In Other Words, What Is More, the Latter: Discourse Markers and Cohesion”. Let’s learn discourse markers for organizing (to begin with, firstly, finally, all in all, to sum up), adding (what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact), reformulating (in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely), contrasting and conceding (on the one hand… on the other hand, while, whereas, admittedly, after all), exemplifying (for instance, a case in point is); spoken markers (well, anyway, mind you); cohesion through reference (this / these / such + summary noun, the former / the latter) and lexical cohesion (synonyms and word families) together. Remember these patterns: In other words, the advertisement is misleading. The argument is not convincing; what is more, it is unfair. What, then, is the solution? Discourse markers tell the reader how ideas connect. Organizing: to begin with, firstly, secondly, finally, all in all, to sum up. Adding: what is more, besides, indeed, in fact, as a matter of fact. Reformulating (saying it again more clearly): in other words, that is to say, to put it another way, namely. Exemplifying: for instance, a case in point is… Contrasting and conceding: on the one hand… on the other hand; while / whereas (inside one sentence); admittedly (accepting a point); after all (giving a reason that the listener should remember: Don't blame him; after all, he's only fourteen.). Spoken markers: well (thinking), anyway (returning to the main topic), mind you (adding a small contrast). Don't use spoken markers in formal essays. Here are some examples. To begin with, the claim has no evidence. The ad uses emotive language; in other words, it tries to frighten us. The speech was long; what is more, it was repetitive. Be careful! Some learners say: “The speech was persuasive. Anyway, it had three main parts. (essay).” That’s not right. We say: “The speech was persuasive. To begin with, it had a clear structure.” No spoken markers in essays. Quick check: Which marker means \"I'll say it again more simply\"? … Think! … *In other words*. Great job! Now you can say: I can use \"in other words\" to explain an idea again more simply.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3","title":"Ethos, Pathos, Logos: The Vocabulary of Rhetoric and Argument","objectives":["Students will be able to analyze persuasive texts with the vocabulary of rhetoric.","Students will be able to identify common logical fallacies and name them.","I can explain ethos, pathos and logos with examples.","I can recognize a false choice or an attack on a person in an argument."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تحليل النصوص الإقناعية بمفردات البلاغة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تحديد المغالطات المنطقية الشائعة وتسميتها.","أستطيع أن أشرح الإيتوس والباثوس واللوغوس بأمثلة.","أستطيع أن أتعرّف على الخيار الزائف أو مهاجمة الشخص في الحجة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"compelling","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kəmˈpɛlɪŋ/","definition":"very convincing and hard to ignore","arabic":"مقنع / قوي","example":"The lawyer presented compelling evidence.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W01"},{"word":"concede","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈsid/","definition":"to admit that something is true, often unwillingly","arabic":"يُقرّ / يسلّم بـ","example":"She conceded that her opponent had a point.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W02"},{"word":"refute","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈfjut/","definition":"to prove that a statement or argument is wrong","arabic":"يفنّد / يدحض","example":"The scientist refuted the claim with new data.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W03"},{"word":"sway","pos":"verb","ipa":"/sweɪ/","definition":"to influence someone's opinion or decision","arabic":"يستميل / يؤثّر في","example":"The final speech swayed the undecided voters.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W04"},{"word":"assertion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈsɝʃən/","definition":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء / تأكيد","example":"His assertion was not supported by evidence.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W05"},{"word":"discourse","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɪskɔrs/","definition":"serious discussion or communication about a subject","arabic":"خطاب / حوار","example":"Public discourse online is often aggressive.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W06"},{"word":"cohesive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/koʊˈhisɪv/","definition":"well connected, so that the parts form a clear whole","arabic":"متماسك","example":"A cohesive essay links each paragraph to the next.","image_id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Aristotle's three appeals: ethos (the speaker's credibility – \"As a doctor with twenty years' experience…\"), pathos (emotion – a moving anecdote, emotive language), logos (logic – reasons, statistics, evidence). A compelling argument usually combines all three. A rhetorical question expects no answer: \"Do we really want our children to breathe polluted air?\"\nArgument words: an assertion (a statement without proof), a counterargument (an opposing argument), a rebuttal (a reply that shows it is wrong); to concede a point (admit it is true), to refute a claim (prove it is wrong), to sway an audience (influence it). Eloquent = speaking beautifully; emotive = causing strong feelings; cohesive = well connected; discourse = serious communication.\nCommon fallacies (false reasoning): attacking the person (\"Don't listen to her – she's too young.\"); false choice (\"Either we ban phones or students will fail.\"); appeal to popularity (\"Millions use it, so it must be good.\"); hasty generalization (\"Two of my friends dislike reading, so teenagers hate books.\"); slippery slope (\"If we allow one snack, soon everyone will eat junk food all day.\").\nCollocations/phrases: make an emotional appeal • a compelling argument • back up a claim with evidence • a rhetorical question | refute a claim • concede a point • sway an audience • a moving anecdote | emotive language • an eloquent speaker • a logical fallacy • a cohesive essay | ethos = credibility • pathos = emotion • logos = logic • public discourse","examples":["The speaker opened with a moving anecdote about her grandmother.","The advertisement makes an emotional appeal to parents.","She backed up her claim with statistics from the World Health Organization.","The scientist refuted the claim with new data.","He conceded the point but continued to argue.","Her final speech swayed the undecided members of the audience.","\"Either you're with us or against us\" is a false choice.","An eloquent speaker chooses every word carefully."],"common_errors":["✗ He refused the claim with evidence. → ✓ He refuted the claim with evidence. (refute = prove wrong.)","✗ The ad uses emotional language to make us cry. (Use the precise term.) → ✓ The ad uses emotive language to make us cry. (emotive = causing strong feelings.)","✗ She conceded that her opponent was wrong. → ✓ She conceded that her opponent was right / had a point. (concede = admit the other side is right.)","✗ Millions of people use this app, so it must be safe. (Is this logical?) → ✓ This is an appeal to popularity; popularity is not proof of safety. (Name the fallacy.)"],"arabic":"استمالات أرسطو الثلاث: ethos (المصداقية) وpathos (العاطفة) وlogos (المنطق)، والحجة المقنعة compelling تجمع بينها.\nكلمات الحجاج: assertion (ادعاء) وcounterargument (حجة مضادة) وrebuttal (تفنيد) وconcede (يُقرّ) وrefute (يدحض) وsway (يستميل).\nالمغالطات الشائعة: مهاجمة الشخص، والخيار الزائف، والاحتكام إلى الشعبية، والتعميم المتسرع، والمنحدر الزلق.\nانتبه: refute تعني يدحض، أما refuse فتعني يرفض."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ethos, Pathos, Logos: The Vocabulary of Rhetoric and Argument”. Let’s learn rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U4-LS3 | Ethos, Pathos, Logos: The Vocabulary of Rhetoric and Argument","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. compelling: very convincing and hard to ignore. concede: to admit that something is true, often unwillingly. refute: to prove that a statement or argument is wrong.","on_screen":"compelling | concede | refute","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Aristotle's three appeals: ethos (the speaker's credibility – \"As a doctor with twenty years' experience…\"), pathos (emotion – a moving anecdote, emotive language), logos (logic – reasons, statistics, evidence). A compelling argument usually combines all three. A rhetorical question expects no answer: \"Do we really want our children to breathe polluted air?\" Argument words: an assertion (a statement without proof), a counterargument (an opposing argument), a rebuttal (a reply that shows it is wrong); to concede a point (admit it is true), to refute a claim (prove it is wrong), to sway an audience (influence it). Eloquent = speaking beautifully; emotive = causing strong feelings; cohesive = well connected; discourse = serious communication.","on_screen":"rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The speaker opened with a moving anecdote about her grandmother. The advertisement makes an emotional appeal to parents. She backed up her claim with statistics from the World Health Organization.","on_screen":"The speaker opened with a moving anecdote about her grandmother. / The advertisement makes an emotional appeal to parents. / She backed up her claim with statistics from the World Health Organization.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “He refused the claim with evidence.” That’s not right. We say: “He refuted the claim with evidence.” refute = prove wrong.","on_screen":"✗ He refused the claim with evidence.   ✓ He refuted the claim with evidence.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which appeal uses statistics: ethos, pathos or logos? … Think! … *logos*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain ethos, pathos and logos with examples.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain ethos, pathos and logos with examples.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","استمالات أرسطو الثلاث: ethos (المصداقية) وpathos (العاطفة) وlogos (المنطق)، والحجة المقنعة compelling تجمع بينها.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: He refused the claim with evidence. — الصحيح: He refuted the claim with evidence.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح الإيتوس والباثوس واللوغوس بأمثلة."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"\"As a doctor with twenty years' experience, I can tell you that sleep matters.\" Which appeal is this?","options":["pathos","a fallacy","ethos","logos"],"answer":"ethos","explanation":"Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"The scientist ___ the claim with new data from three experiments.","options":["swayed","conceded","refused","refuted"],"answer":"refuted","explanation":"Rhetoric word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"She opened her speech with a moving ___ about her grandfather.","options":["rebuttal","anecdote","fallacy","assertion"],"answer":"anecdote","explanation":"Rhetoric collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"\"Either we ban all phones or students will fail every exam.\" This is an example of ___.","options":["a false choice","ethos","a rebuttal","an appeal to emotion"],"answer":"a false choice","explanation":"Name the fallacy.","explanation_ar":"المغالطة."},{"q":"Her final speech ___ the undecided members of the audience.","options":["refuted","swayed","asserted","conceded"],"answer":"swayed","explanation":"Rhetoric word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Although the debater disagreed with most of her opponent's points, she ______ that his figures on youth unemployment were accurate.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["swayed","denied","conceded","refuted"],"answer":"conceded","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"rebuttal","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"a reply showing that an argument is wrong","explanation":"rebuttal → a reply showing that an argument is wrong. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"assertion","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"a statement made without proof","explanation":"assertion → a statement made without proof. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"eloquent","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"able to speak clearly and beautifully","explanation":"eloquent → able to speak clearly and beautifully. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"pathos","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"an appeal to the audience's emotions","explanation":"pathos → an appeal to the audience's emotions. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"logos","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"an appeal to logic and evidence","explanation":"logos → an appeal to logic and evidence. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"slippery slope","options":["an appeal to logic and evidence","able to speak clearly and beautifully","the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","an appeal to the audience's emotions","a reply showing that an argument is wrong","a statement made without proof"],"answer":"the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results","explanation":"slippery slope → the claim that one small step will lead to extreme results. Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Analyze persuasion.","items":[{"q":"Describe an advertisement you have seen. Which appeal does it use?","options":[],"answer":"A car advertisement shows a happy family on a trip; it uses pathos.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a rhetorical question for a speech about recycling.","options":[],"answer":"Do we really want to leave our children a planet full of plastic?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence that concedes a point.","options":[],"answer":"I concede that online learning is flexible, but it cannot replace classroom discussion.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write an example of an appeal to popularity.","options":[],"answer":"Everyone is buying this phone, so it must be the best.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Why should we avoid attacking the person in a debate?","options":[],"answer":"It doesn't prove the argument wrong; it only distracts the audience.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Analyze a speech.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences analyzing a speech or ad using \"ethos\", \"pathos\" and \"logos\".","options":[],"answer":"The speaker builds ethos by mentioning her experience as a nurse. She uses pathos when she describes a sick child. She uses logos when she gives hospital statistics.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a short rebuttal to this claim: \"Video games make all teenagers violent.\"","options":[],"answer":"This is a hasty generalization; most studies have found no clear link between video games and violent behavior.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"compelling\".","options":[],"answer":"The documentary presented compelling evidence that plastic is harming marine life.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Dominoes","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Deal the dominoes equally in teams of four.","Player 1 places a domino; the next player adds a domino whose word matches the open definition.","If you cannot play, pass.","The first player with no dominoes left wins."],"data":[{"word":"rhetoric","match":"the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people","arabic":"البلاغة / فن الخطابة"},{"word":"anecdote","match":"a short, interesting story about a real event or person","arabic":"حكاية قصيرة / طرفة"},{"word":"appeal","match":"an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust","arabic":"مناشدة / استمالة"},{"word":"counterargument","match":"an argument that opposes another argument","arabic":"حجة مضادة"},{"word":"rebuttal","match":"a response that shows an argument is wrong","arabic":"تفنيد / رد"},{"word":"fallacy","match":"a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning","arabic":"مغالطة"},{"word":"emotive","match":"causing strong feelings","arabic":"مثير للعواطف"},{"word":"eloquent","match":"able to speak or write clearly and beautifully","arabic":"فصيح / بليغ"},{"word":"compelling","match":"very convincing and hard to ignore","arabic":"مقنع / قوي"},{"word":"concede","match":"to admit that something is true, often unwillingly","arabic":"يُقرّ / يسلّم بـ"},{"word":"refute","match":"to prove that a statement or argument is wrong","arabic":"يفنّد / يدحض"},{"word":"sway","match":"to influence someone's opinion or decision","arabic":"يستميل / يؤثّر في"},{"word":"assertion","match":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء / تأكيد"},{"word":"discourse","match":"serious discussion or communication about a subject","arabic":"خطاب / حوار"},{"word":"cohesive","match":"well connected, so that the parts form a clear whole","arabic":"متماسك"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"\"Ninety percent of patients improved after the treatment.\" Which appeal is this?","options":["logos","pathos","ethos","a fallacy"],"answer":"logos","explanation":"Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"A picture of a crying child in an advertisement is mainly an appeal to ___.","options":["popularity","credibility (ethos)","logic (logos)","emotion (pathos)"],"answer":"emotion (pathos)","explanation":"Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"\"Don't listen to his ideas about education – he isn't even a teacher.\" This fallacy is ___.","options":["a slippery slope","appeal to logic","a false choice","attacking the person"],"answer":"attacking the person","explanation":"Name the fallacy.","explanation_ar":"المغالطة."},{"q":"\"If we let students use calculators, soon they won't be able to think at all.\" This is ___.","options":["ethos","a slippery slope","a rebuttal","a counterargument"],"answer":"a slippery slope","explanation":"Name the fallacy.","explanation_ar":"المغالطة."},{"q":"The lawyer presented ___ evidence, and the jury was convinced.","options":["compelling","cohesive","eloquent","emotive"],"answer":"compelling","explanation":"Rhetoric collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"A strong essay always answers the main ___.","options":["eloquence","anecdote","counterargument","discourse"],"answer":"counterargument","explanation":"Rhetoric word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The article uses ___ language, such as \"disaster\" and \"catastrophe\", to shock readers.","options":["emotive","compelling","eloquent","cohesive"],"answer":"emotive","explanation":"Rhetoric collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Public ___ on social media is often aggressive and impolite.","options":["anecdote","discourse","fallacy","rebuttal"],"answer":"discourse","explanation":"Rhetoric word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Rather than simply stating that the policy would fail, the economist ______ her claim with data from four countries that had tried similar policies.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["conceded","refused","swayed","backed up"],"answer":"backed up","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The essay was praised for being ______: each paragraph followed logically from the one before, and every example clearly supported the main argument.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["fallacious","eloquent in tone","cohesive","emotive"],"answer":"cohesive","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A rhetoric workshop: a triangle diagram with \"Ethos – trust\", \"Pathos – emotion\", \"Logos – logic\"; three advertisement posters on the wall, each labeled with the appeal it uses; a \"Fallacy Detector\" checklist on a whiteboard. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A rhetoric workshop: a triangle diagram with \"Ethos – trust\", \"Pathos – emotion\", \"Logos – logic\"; three advertisement posters on the wall, each labeled with the appeal it uses; a \"Fallacy Detector\" checklist on a whiteboard"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows compelling (very convincing and hard to ignore); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing compelling: very convincing and hard to ignore"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows concede (to admit that something is true, often unwillingly); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing concede: to admit that something is true, often unwillingly"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows refute (to prove that a statement or argument is wrong); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing refute: to prove that a statement or argument is wrong"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows sway (to influence someone's opinion or decision); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing sway: to influence someone's opinion or decision"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows assertion (a statement that something is true, often without proof); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing assertion: a statement that something is true, often without proof"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows discourse (serious discussion or communication about a subject); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing discourse: serious discussion or communication about a subject"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows cohesive (well connected, so that the parts form a clear whole); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing cohesive: well connected, so that the parts form a clear whole"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Dominoes”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Dominoes"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W01","script":"compelling. compelling. The lawyer presented compelling evidence.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W02","script":"concede. concede. She conceded that her opponent had a point.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W03","script":"refute. refute. The scientist refuted the claim with new data.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W04","script":"sway. sway. The final speech swayed the undecided voters.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W05","script":"assertion. assertion. His assertion was not supported by evidence.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W06","script":"discourse. discourse. Public discourse online is often aggressive.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-W07","script":"cohesive. cohesive. A cohesive essay links each paragraph to the next.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ethos, Pathos, Logos: The Vocabulary of Rhetoric and Argument”. Let’s learn rhetoric vocabulary (rhetoric, appeal, anecdote, counterargument, rebuttal, fallacy, emotive, eloquent, compelling, concede, refute, sway, assertion, discourse, cohesive); Aristotle's appeals (ethos – credibility, pathos – emotion, logos – logic); common fallacies (attacking the person, false choice, appeal to popularity, hasty generalization, slippery slope); collocations (make an emotional appeal, a compelling argument, refute a claim, concede a point, sway an audience, a rhetorical question, a moving anecdote, emotive language, back up a claim with evidence) together. Meet some new words. compelling: very convincing and hard to ignore. concede: to admit that something is true, often unwillingly. refute: to prove that a statement or argument is wrong. Aristotle's three appeals: ethos (the speaker's credibility – \"As a doctor with twenty years' experience…\"), pathos (emotion – a moving anecdote, emotive language), logos (logic – reasons, statistics, evidence). A compelling argument usually combines all three. A rhetorical question expects no answer: \"Do we really want our children to breathe polluted air?\" Argument words: an assertion (a statement without proof), a counterargument (an opposing argument), a rebuttal (a reply that shows it is wrong); to concede a point (admit it is true), to refute a claim (prove it is wrong), to sway an audience (influence it). Eloquent = speaking beautifully; emotive = causing strong feelings; cohesive = well connected; discourse = serious communication. Here are some examples. The speaker opened with a moving anecdote about her grandmother. The advertisement makes an emotional appeal to parents. She backed up her claim with statistics from the World Health Organization. Be careful! Some learners say: “He refused the claim with evidence.” That’s not right. We say: “He refuted the claim with evidence.” refute = prove wrong. Quick check: Which appeal uses statistics: ethos, pathos or logos? … Think! … *logos*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain ethos, pathos and logos with examples.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4","title":"When Sara Met Lina, She…: Pronoun Clarity and Ambiguity","objectives":["Students will be able to recognize and fix ambiguous and vague pronouns.","Students will be able to use its for organizations and collective nouns and replace pronouns that have no antecedent.","I can rewrite \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous\" so that it is clear who was nervous.","I can write \"The committee announced its decision.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعرف على الضمائر الغامضة والمبهمة وإصلاحها.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام its مع المؤسسات والأسماء الجماعية واستبدال الضمائر التي ليس لها مرجع.","أستطيع أن أعيد كتابة When Sara met Lina, she was nervous بحيث يتضح من كانت متوترة.","أستطيع أن أكتب The committee announced its decision."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"rhetoric","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛtɚɪk/","definition":"the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people","arabic":"البلاغة / فن الخطابة","example":"Students of rhetoric study famous speeches.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"anecdote","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænəkˌdoʊt/","definition":"a short, interesting story about a real event or person","arabic":"حكاية قصيرة / طرفة","example":"The speaker began with a funny anecdote.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"appeal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpil/","definition":"an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust","arabic":"مناشدة / استمالة","example":"The advertisement makes an emotional appeal.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"counterargument","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊntɚˌɑrɡjəmənt/","definition":"an argument that opposes another argument","arabic":"حجة مضادة","example":"A good essay answers the main counterargument.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"rebuttal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈbʌtəl/","definition":"a response that shows an argument is wrong","arabic":"تفنيد / رد","example":"Her rebuttal was clear and well supported.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"fallacy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfæləsi/","definition":"a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning","arabic":"مغالطة","example":"Attacking the speaker instead of the argument is a common fallacy.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"A pronoun must point clearly to one antecedent. Ambiguous: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous. (Who was nervous?) → When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / Sara was nervous when she met Lina. Ambiguous: The speaker told the student that his argument was weak. → The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\"\nVague this / that / which / it: when the pronoun refers to a whole idea, add a summary noun: The website hid the author's name, and this worried readers. → This lack of transparency worried readers. The speaker repeated himself, which annoyed the audience → a habit that annoyed the audience. No antecedent: (not: In the article, it says) → The article says. (not: They say that) → Experts say that.\nOrganizations and collective nouns (the company, the committee, the government, the team, the audience) take its in standard American English: The committee announced its decision. (NOT (not: their) in formal SAT-style writing.) Keep one point of view: don't switch from students to you: (not: When students debate, you should listen.) → When students debate, they should listen.\none pronoun → one clear antecedent (repeat the noun if needed)\nthis / that / which / it + summary noun (this decision, this lack of evidence)\nno antecedent → name the source (The article says…, Experts say…)\ncompany / committee / government / team → its","examples":["When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous.","The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\"","The website hid its author's name. This lack of transparency worried readers.","The article says that the claim is false.","Experts say that the study is reliable.","The committee announced its decision on Monday.","The company changed its advertising strategy.","When students debate, they should listen carefully."],"common_errors":["✗ When Omar talked to his father, he was angry. → ✓ When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry. (Two possible antecedents.)","✗ In the report, it says that sales fell. → ✓ The report says that sales fell. (No antecedent for it.)","✗ The team lost their first debate. (formal) → ✓ The team lost its first debate. (Collective noun → its.)","✗ The ad exaggerated the benefits and hid the risks. This was unfair. → ✓ The ad exaggerated the benefits and hid the risks. This misleading approach was unfair. (Summary noun.)"],"arabic":"يجب أن يشير الضمير بوضوح إلى مرجع واحد: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous جملة غامضة، فنكرر الاسم: Sara was nervous.\nإذا أشار this / which / it إلى فكرة كاملة نضيف اسمًا ملخِّصًا: This lack of transparency worried readers.\nإذا لم يكن للضمير مرجع نسمّي المصدر: The article says… / Experts say… بدلًا من In the article, it says… / They say…\nالمؤسسات والأسماء الجماعية مثل company وcommittee وteam تأخذ its في الإنجليزية الأمريكية الرسمية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “When Sara Met Lina, She…: Pronoun Clarity and Ambiguity”. Let’s learn clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U4-LS4 | When Sara Met Lina, She…: Pronoun Clarity and Ambiguity","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"A pronoun must point clearly to one antecedent. Ambiguous: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous. (Who was nervous?) → When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / Sara was nervous when she met Lina. Ambiguous: The speaker told the student that his argument was weak. → The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\"","on_screen":"clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A pronoun must point clearly to one antecedent. Ambiguous: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous. (Who was nervous?) → When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / Sara was nervous when she met Lina. Ambiguous: The speaker told the student that his argument was weak. → The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" Vague this / that / which / it: when the pronoun refers to a whole idea, add a summary noun: The website hid the author's name, and this worried readers. → This lack of transparency worried readers. The speaker repeated himself, which annoyed the audience → a habit that annoyed the audience. No antecedent: (not: In the article, it says) → The article says. (not: They say that) → Experts say that.","on_screen":"one pronoun → one clear antecedent (repeat the noun if needed) • this / that / which / it + summary noun (this decision, this lack of evidence) • no antecedent → name the source (The article says…, Experts say…)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" The website hid its author's name. This lack of transparency worried readers.","on_screen":"When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" / The website hid its author's name. This lack of transparency worried readers.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “When Omar talked to his father, he was angry.” That’s not right. We say: “When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry.” Two possible antecedents.","on_screen":"✗ When Omar talked to his father, he was angry.   ✓ When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous.\" Who was nervous? … Think! … We can't tell – it's ambiguous. Great job! Now you can say: I can rewrite \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous\" so that it is clear who was nervous.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can rewrite \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous\" so that it is clear who was nervous.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","يجب أن يشير الضمير بوضوح إلى مرجع واحد: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous جملة غامضة، فنكرر الاسم: Sara was nervous.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: When Omar talked to his father, he was angry. — الصحيح: When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أعيد كتابة When Sara met Lina, she was nervous بحيث يتضح من كانت متوترة."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Debate Report (editing passage)","body":"The school debate club held their final competition last week. When Hamad faced Rakan in the final round, he seemed nervous at first. In the rules, it says that each speaker has three minutes. Rakan spoke for five minutes. This cost his team points. In the end, the judges praised both speakers, and the club thanked them for their time.","word_count":60,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the clearest answer.","items":[{"q":"","options":["When Sara met her, she was nervous about the debate.","When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous about the debate.","When Sara met Lina, she was nervous about the debate.","When she met Lina, she was nervous about the debate."],"answer":"When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous about the debate.","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"","options":["It says in it that the claim is false.","In the article, they say that the claim is false.","In the article, it says that the claim is false.","The article says that the claim is false."],"answer":"The article says that the claim is false.","explanation":"Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"ضمير بلا مرجع."},{"q":"","options":["The committee announced their decision on Monday.","The committee announced it's decision on Monday.","The committee announced they're decision on Monday.","The committee announced its decision on Monday."],"answer":"The committee announced its decision on Monday.","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"The website hid the name of its author. ___ worried many readers.","options":["This lack of transparency","Which","It","This"],"answer":"This lack of transparency","explanation":"Vague this / which / it → summary noun.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة المبهمة."},{"q":"","options":["When students debate, they should listen carefully.","When students debate, you should listen carefully.","When students debate, we should listen carefully to them.","When students debate, one should listen carefully to you."],"answer":"When students debate, they should listen carefully.","explanation":"Consistent point of view.","explanation_ar":"ثبات الضمير."},{"q":"The advertising company was fined after ______ misleading claims about a children's medicine were investigated by the government.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["they're","it's","its","their"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite to make the pronoun clear.","items":[{"q":"When Omar talked to his father, he was angry. (Omar was angry)  (clear antecedent)","options":[],"answer":"When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry.","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"They say that sugar is bad for your teeth. (Dentists)  (name the source)","options":[],"answer":"Dentists say that sugar is bad for your teeth.","explanation":"Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"ضمير بلا مرجع."},{"q":"The speaker repeated the same joke three times, which annoyed the audience. (a habit that)  (summary noun)","options":[],"answer":"The speaker repeated the same joke three times, a habit that annoyed the audience.","explanation":"Vague this / which / it → summary noun.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة المبهمة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the pronoun.","items":[{"q":"The team lost their first debate of the season.","options":[],"answer":"The team lost its first debate of the season.","explanation":"their → its. Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: their → its. الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"In the report, it says that sales fell by ten percent.","options":[],"answer":"The report says that sales fell by ten percent.","explanation":"In the report, it says → The report says. Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: In the report, it says → The report says. ضمير بلا مرجع."},{"q":"The ad exaggerated the benefits and hid the risks. This was unfair.","options":[],"answer":"The ad exaggerated the benefits and hid the risks. This misleading approach was unfair.","explanation":"This → This misleading approach. Vague this / which / it → summary noun.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: This → This misleading approach. الإحالة المبهمة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the debate report.","items":[{"q":"The school debate club held their final competition last week.","options":[],"answer":"The school debate club held its final competition last week.","explanation":"their → its. Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: their → its. الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"When Hamad faced Rakan in the final round, he seemed nervous at first. The rules say that each speaker has three minutes.","options":[],"answer":"When Hamad faced Rakan in the final round, Hamad seemed nervous at first. The rules say that each speaker has three minutes.","explanation":"he → Hamad. Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: he → Hamad. الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"When Hamad faced Rakan in the final round, Hamad seemed nervous at first. In the rules, it says that each speaker has three minutes.","options":[],"answer":"When Hamad faced Rakan in the final round, Hamad seemed nervous at first. The rules say that each speaker has three minutes.","explanation":"In the rules, it says → The rules say. Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: In the rules, it says → The rules say. الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"Rakan spoke for five minutes. This cost his team points.","options":[],"answer":"Rakan spoke for five minutes. This rule violation cost his team points.","explanation":"This → This rule violation. Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: This → This rule violation. الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"In the end, the judges praised both speakers, and the club thanked them for their time.","options":[],"answer":"In the end, the judges praised both speakers, and the club thanked the judges for their time.","explanation":"them → the judges. Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: them → the judges. الضمير الغامض."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write clear sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence about two people where the pronoun is clear.","options":[],"answer":"After Sara helped Lina, Lina thanked her.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence about a company using \"its\".","options":[],"answer":"The company apologized for its misleading advertisement.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two sentences connected by \"This + summary noun\".","options":[],"answer":"Some influencers don't reveal that they are paid. This lack of honesty damages trust.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Speed Spell","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a word and a short sentence with it.","Students write the word in 10 seconds.","Show boards on \"Show me!\". Correct = 1 point.","After 10 words, partners check each other's boards and correct any mistakes."],"data":[{"word":"rhetoric","clue":"the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people"},{"word":"anecdote","clue":"a short, interesting story about a real event or person"},{"word":"counterargument","clue":"an argument that opposes another argument"},{"word":"rebuttal","clue":"a response that shows an argument is wrong"},{"word":"eloquent","clue":"able to speak or write clearly and beautifully"},{"word":"discourse","clue":"serious discussion or communication about a subject"},{"word":"appeal","clue":"an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust"},{"word":"fallacy","clue":"a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning"},{"word":"emotive","clue":"causing strong feelings"},{"word":"compelling","clue":"very convincing and hard to ignore"},{"word":"concede","clue":"to admit that something is true, often unwillingly"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The government changed its policy on advertising to children.","The government changed their policy on advertising to children.","The government changed it's policy on advertising to children.","The government changed there policy on advertising to children."],"answer":"The government changed its policy on advertising to children.","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"","options":["It says that the study was flawed.","Researchers say that the study was flawed.","They say that the study was flawed.","In it, they say that the study was flawed."],"answer":"Researchers say that the study was flawed.","explanation":"Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"ضمير بلا مرجع."},{"q":"","options":["Sara told her that she was excellent.","She told her that her speech was excellent.","Sara told Noura that her speech was excellent.","Sara told Noura, \"Your speech was excellent.\""],"answer":"Sara told Noura, \"Your speech was excellent.\"","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"","options":["The speaker ignored every question, and it angered them.","The speaker ignored every question, which angered them.","The speaker ignored every question, a decision that angered the audience.","The speaker ignored every question, this angered the audience."],"answer":"The speaker ignored every question, a decision that angered the audience.","explanation":"Vague this / which / it → summary noun.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة المبهمة."},{"q":"","options":["If students want to win, they must prepare carefully.","If students want to win, you must prepare carefully.","If a student wants to win, you must prepare carefully.","If students want to win, one must prepare them carefully."],"answer":"If students want to win, they must prepare carefully.","explanation":"Consistent point of view.","explanation_ar":"ثبات الضمير."},{"q":"The jury reached ___ verdict after three hours.","options":["they're","its","it's","their"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"The newspaper published the story without checking it. ___ led to a public apology.","options":["It","Which","This","This mistake"],"answer":"This mistake","explanation":"Vague this / which / it → summary noun.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة المبهمة."},{"q":"After reviewing complaints from hundreds of customers, the airline announced that ______ would refund all passengers whose flights had been canceled.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["it","they","he","one"],"answer":"it","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"When the debate coach spoke to the new student, ______ explained the rules of the competition in detail.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["she","the coach","they","it"],"answer":"the coach","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"Which sentence has a pronoun with no antecedent?","options":["The speech says that education is a right.","According to the speech, education is a right.","In the speech, it says that education is a right.","The speaker says that education is a right."],"answer":"In the speech, it says that education is a right.","explanation":"Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"ضمير بلا مرجع."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “The Power of Persuasion” that shows clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “The Power of Persuasion” that shows clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent in action"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Speed Spell”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Speed Spell"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “When Sara Met Lina, She…: Pronoun Clarity and Ambiguity”. Let’s learn clear pronoun reference: ambiguous pronouns with two possible antecedents (When Sara met Lina, she… → name the person); vague this / that / which / it referring to a whole idea (add a summary noun: this decision); pronouns with no antecedent (In the article, it says… → The article says…; They say… → Experts say…); collective nouns and organizations with its (The committee announced its decision); keeping pronoun reference consistent together. A pronoun must point clearly to one antecedent. Ambiguous: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous. (Who was nervous?) → When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / Sara was nervous when she met Lina. Ambiguous: The speaker told the student that his argument was weak. → The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" A pronoun must point clearly to one antecedent. Ambiguous: When Sara met Lina, she was nervous. (Who was nervous?) → When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. / Sara was nervous when she met Lina. Ambiguous: The speaker told the student that his argument was weak. → The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" Vague this / that / which / it: when the pronoun refers to a whole idea, add a summary noun: The website hid the author's name, and this worried readers. → This lack of transparency worried readers. The speaker repeated himself, which annoyed the audience → a habit that annoyed the audience. No antecedent: (not: In the article, it says) → The article says. (not: They say that) → Experts say that. Here are some examples. When Sara met Lina, Sara was nervous. The speaker told the student, \"Your argument is weak.\" The website hid its author's name. This lack of transparency worried readers. Be careful! Some learners say: “When Omar talked to his father, he was angry.” That’s not right. We say: “When Omar talked to his father, Omar was angry.” Two possible antecedents. Quick check: \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous.\" Who was nervous? … Think! … We can't tell – it's ambiguous. Great job! Now you can say: I can rewrite \"When Sara met Lina, she was nervous\" so that it is clear who was nervous.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5","title":"A Speech That Moves People: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to deliver a short persuasive speech using ethos, pathos and logos.","Students will be able to write a persuasive speech with cohesive markers, substitution and clear pronouns, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can deliver a persuasive speech with all three appeals.","I can write a cohesive persuasive speech."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من إلقاء كلمة إقناعية قصيرة باستخدام الإيتوس والباثوس واللوغوس.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة كلمة إقناعية بأدوات التماسك والاستبدال والضمائر الواضحة، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن ألقي كلمة إقناعية بالاستمالات الثلاث.","أستطيع أن أكتب كلمة إقناعية متماسكة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"rhetoric","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛtɚɪk/","definition":"the art of using language effectively to persuade or influence people","arabic":"البلاغة / فن الخطابة","example":"Students of rhetoric study famous speeches.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"anecdote","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænəkˌdoʊt/","definition":"a short, interesting story about a real event or person","arabic":"حكاية قصيرة / طرفة","example":"The speaker began with a funny anecdote.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"appeal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpil/","definition":"an attempt to persuade by using emotions, logic or trust","arabic":"مناشدة / استمالة","example":"The advertisement makes an emotional appeal.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"counterargument","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊntɚˌɑrɡjəmənt/","definition":"an argument that opposes another argument","arabic":"حجة مضادة","example":"A good essay answers the main counterargument.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"rebuttal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈbʌtəl/","definition":"a response that shows an argument is wrong","arabic":"تفنيد / رد","example":"Her rebuttal was clear and well supported.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"fallacy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfæləsi/","definition":"a mistaken belief or a false way of reasoning","arabic":"مغالطة","example":"Attacking the speaker instead of the argument is a common fallacy.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"emotive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ɪˈmoʊtɪv/","definition":"causing strong feelings","arabic":"مثير للعواطف","example":"The article uses emotive language to shock readers.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"eloquent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛləkwənt/","definition":"able to speak or write clearly and beautifully","arabic":"فصيح / بليغ","example":"He gave an eloquent speech at graduation.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"compelling","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kəmˈpɛlɪŋ/","definition":"very convincing and hard to ignore","arabic":"مقنع / قوي","example":"The lawyer presented compelling evidence.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"concede","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈsid/","definition":"to admit that something is true, often unwillingly","arabic":"يُقرّ / يسلّم بـ","example":"She conceded that her opponent had a point.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"refute","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈfjut/","definition":"to prove that a statement or argument is wrong","arabic":"يفنّد / يدحض","example":"The scientist refuted the claim with new data.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sway","pos":"verb","ipa":"/sweɪ/","definition":"to influence someone's opinion or decision","arabic":"يستميل / يؤثّر في","example":"The final speech swayed the undecided voters.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"assertion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈsɝʃən/","definition":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء / تأكيد","example":"His assertion was not supported by evidence.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"discourse","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɪskɔrs/","definition":"serious discussion or communication about a subject","arabic":"خطاب / حوار","example":"Public discourse online is often aggressive.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cohesive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/koʊˈhisɪv/","definition":"well connected, so that the parts form a clear whole","arabic":"متماسك","example":"A cohesive essay links each paragraph to the next.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 4: substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and clear pronoun reference.\nA persuasive speech opens with credibility or a striking story, develops reasons with evidence, answers a counterargument (a rebuttal), and ends with a call to action. Discourse markers guide listeners; substitution keeps the speech flowing; clear pronouns prevent confusion.\n✔ ethos (As a volunteer…) • pathos (a moving anecdote) • logos (statistics)\n✔ I don't think so • many families already do so • others don't\n✔ in other words • what is more • however • after all\n✔ this + summary noun • its for organizations • one clear antecedent","examples":["As a volunteer for two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes. (ethos)","I met a mother who had not eaten for two days. (pathos)","Millions of tons of food are thrown away every year. (logos)","Can we accept this situation? I don't think so.","In other words, small actions add up.","Many families already do so.","The food bank has opened its doors to everyone.","However, this argument ignores the power of small actions."],"common_errors":["✗ Can we accept this? I don't think it. → ✓ Can we accept this situation? I don't think so. (Summary noun + so.)","✗ The food bank needs volunteers for their new program. → ✓ The food bank needs volunteers for its new program. (Organization → its.)","✗ Anyway, let me give you some statistics. (speech) → ✓ To begin with, let me give you some statistics. (Formal marker.)"],"arabic":"نراجع الاستبدال والحذف وأدوات الخطاب والتماسك ومفردات البلاغة والضمائر الواضحة.\nالكلمة الإقناعية تبدأ بالمصداقية أو بقصة مؤثرة، وتعرض أسبابًا بأدلة، وترد على الحجة المضادة، وتنتهي بدعوة للعمل.\nأدوات الخطاب ترشد المستمعين، والاستبدال يجعل الكلمة سلسة، والضمائر الواضحة تمنع الالتباس."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Speech That Moves People: Language in Use”. Let’s learn substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U4-LS5 | A Speech That Moves People: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Persuasion challenge: prepare a 2-minute speech on a cause you care about (food waste, reading, kindness online, water saving). Include one example each of ethos, pathos and logos, one rhetorical question, two discourse markers, one substitution (so / not / do so) and one rebuttal of a counterargument. The audience identifies the three appeals.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 4: substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and clear pronoun reference. A persuasive speech opens with credibility or a striking story, develops reasons with evidence, answers a counterargument (a rebuttal), and ends with a call to action. Discourse markers guide listeners; substitution keeps the speech flowing; clear pronouns prevent confusion.","on_screen":"✔ ethos (As a volunteer…) • pathos (a moving anecdote) • logos (statistics) • ✔ I don't think so • many families already do so • others don't • ✔ in other words • what is more • however • after all","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. As a volunteer for two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes. (ethos) I met a mother who had not eaten for two days. (pathos) Millions of tons of food are thrown away every year. (logos)","on_screen":"As a volunteer for two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes. (ethos) / I met a mother who had not eaten for two days. (pathos) / Millions of tons of food are thrown away every year. (logos)","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Can we accept this? I don't think it.” That’s not right. We say: “Can we accept this situation? I don't think so.” Summary noun + so.","on_screen":"✗ Can we accept this? I don't think it.   ✓ Can we accept this situation? I don't think so.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “The food bank needs volunteers for their new program.”? … Think! … It should be “The food bank needs volunteers for its new program.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can deliver a persuasive speech with all three appeals.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can deliver a persuasive speech with all three appeals.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع الاستبدال والحذف وأدوات الخطاب والتماسك ومفردات البلاغة والضمائر الواضحة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Can we accept this? I don't think it. — الصحيح: Can we accept this situation? I don't think so.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن ألقي كلمة إقناعية بالاستمالات الثلاث."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Waste Less, Share More – Adam","body":"As a volunteer at our local food bank for the past two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes.\nLast winter, I met a mother who had not eaten for two days so that her children could.\nMeanwhile, millions of tons of food are thrown away every year around the world.\nCan we really accept this situation? I don't think so.\nSome people argue that one family's waste makes no difference; however, this argument ignores the power of small actions.\nIn other words, if every family saves a little, the total will be enormous.\nWhat is more, food banks in our city make it easy to donate, and many families already do so.\nLet us waste less and share more, starting today.","word_count":124,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Adam's speech. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Adam has volunteered at a food bank for two years.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He tells a story about a mother who gave her food to her children.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Adam says very little food is thrown away around the world.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He thinks we should accept the situation.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Adam rebuts the argument that one family's waste makes no difference.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"According to Adam, no families donate to food banks yet.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Waste Less, Share More – Adam”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"\"As a volunteer at our local food bank…\" Which appeal does this sentence use?","options":["logos","a fallacy","ethos","pathos"],"answer":"ethos","explanation":"Rhetoric vocabulary or appeal.","explanation_ar":"البلاغة."},{"q":"Can we really accept this situation? I don't think ___.","options":["it","so","not","that"],"answer":"so","explanation":"Substitution or ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"الاستبدال والحذف."},{"q":"Food banks make it easy to donate, and many families already ___.","options":["make so","do so","so do","do it so"],"answer":"do so","explanation":"Substitution or ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"الاستبدال والحذف."},{"q":"___, if every family saves a little, the total will be enormous.","options":["Namely","Mind you","In other words","Anyway"],"answer":"In other words","explanation":"Discourse marker or cohesion.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الخطاب."},{"q":"The food bank has opened ___ doors to everyone in the city.","options":["its","their","they're","it's"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Clear pronoun reference.","explanation_ar":"وضوح الضمير."},{"q":"Some people believe that one family's food waste makes no difference. ______ the waste of millions of families together adds up to millions of tons each year.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","Therefore,","For this reason,","In reality,"],"answer":"In reality,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Plan your speech.","items":[{"q":"Your cause and an ethos sentence: ___","options":[],"answer":"Reading: As someone who has read a book every week for three years, I know how reading changes lives.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A pathos sentence (a short anecdote): ___","options":[],"answer":"My younger brother struggled at school until he discovered stories about space.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A logos sentence (evidence): ___","options":[],"answer":"Studies show that students who read for pleasure have larger vocabularies.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A rhetorical question and a short answer with substitution: ___","options":[],"answer":"Is reading only for exams? I hope not.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A rebuttal of a counterargument: ___","options":[],"answer":"Some say there's no time to read; however, ten minutes a day is enough to start.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your persuasive speech.","items":[{"q":"Plan: ethos opening, anecdote, evidence, rhetorical question, counterargument + rebuttal, call to action.","options":[],"answer":"Kindness online; I moderate a student forum; a friend left social media after cruel comments; surveys on cyberbullying; \"Would you say it face to face?\"; \"it's just a joke\" – rebuttal; think before you post","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a persuasive speech for the cause you chose (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"As a moderator of our school's online forum, I read hundreds of comments every week. Last year, a close friend deleted all her accounts after receiving cruel messages about her appearance. Surveys suggest that many teenagers have experienced online bullying. Would we say these words to someone's face? I don't think so. Some people argue that mean comments are just jokes; however, this excuse ignores the real pain they cause. In other words, a joke that hurts someone is not a joke. What is more, every platform allows us to report abuse, and responsible users do so. Let us think before we post, because kindness costs nothing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: three appeals? substitution correct? any pronoun with two possible antecedents? Fix one problem.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"When my friend told her mother, she cried\" to \"When my friend told her mother, my friend cried\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin the Review Wheel","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["A player spins the wheel and lands on a word.","Say what the word means and use it in a sentence with substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","Teammates check: meaning (1 point), grammar (1 point), pronunciation (1 point).","Highest total after 6 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"rhetoric","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"anecdote","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"appeal","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"counterargument","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"rebuttal","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"fallacy","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"emotive","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"eloquent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"compelling","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"concede","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"refute","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"sway","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"assertion","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"discourse","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"cohesive","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"\"Ninety percent of food waste happens in homes and shops.\" This sentence mainly uses ___.","options":["pathos","a fallacy","logos","ethos"],"answer":"logos","explanation":"Rhetoric vocabulary or appeal.","explanation_ar":"البلاغة."},{"q":"\"Will people change their habits?\" \"I hope ___.\"","options":["so","yes","it","that"],"answer":"so","explanation":"Substitution or ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"الاستبدال والحذف."},{"q":"Some speakers use notes; others ___.","options":["haven't use","don't","aren't","not"],"answer":"don't","explanation":"Substitution or ellipsis.","explanation_ar":"الاستبدال والحذف."},{"q":"The speech was moving; ___, it was full of useful facts.","options":["namely","anyway","in other words","what is more"],"answer":"what is more","explanation":"Discourse marker or cohesion.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الخطاب."},{"q":"","options":["The charity thanked there volunteers at the end of the year.","The charity thanked their volunteers at the end of the year.","The charity thanked it's volunteers at the end of the year.","The charity thanked its volunteers at the end of the year."],"answer":"The charity thanked its volunteers at the end of the year.","explanation":"Clear pronoun reference.","explanation_ar":"وضوح الضمير."},{"q":"The speaker began with statistics about water use. ______ she told the story of a village that had run out of water, which moved many listeners.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For this reason,","Then,","However,","In contrast,"],"answer":"Then,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Many advertisements rely on emotional appeals. ______ others focus on facts such as price and performance.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["As a result,","By contrast,","Namely,","Therefore,"],"answer":"By contrast,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Aristotle described three types of persuasive appeal.\n• Ethos is based on the speaker's credibility.\n• Pathos is based on emotion.\n• Logos is based on logic and evidence.\n\nThe student wants to explain the difference between pathos and logos. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Aristotle described three types of appeal.","Ethos is based on credibility.","While pathos persuades through emotion, logos persuades through logic and evidence.","Persuasion is important."],"answer":"While pathos persuades through emotion, logos persuades through logic and evidence.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Al-Jahiz was an Arab scholar of the ninth century.\n• He lived in Basra.\n• He wrote a famous book about eloquence and clear expression.\n• His work influenced later writers on rhetoric.\n\nThe student wants to introduce Al-Jahiz to an audience unfamiliar with him. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Many scholars wrote books.","His work influenced later writers.","He lived in Basra.","Al-Jahiz, a ninth-century Arab scholar from Basra, wrote a famous book about eloquence that influenced later writers on rhetoric."],"answer":"Al-Jahiz, a ninth-century Arab scholar from Basra, wrote a famous book about eloquence that influenced later writers on rhetoric.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best closing sentence for a persuasive speech:","options":["Let us waste less and share more, starting today.","Some do; others not.","Anyway, that's it, I guess.","In the article, it says we should waste less."],"answer":"Let us waste less and share more, starting today.","explanation":"Rhetoric vocabulary or appeal.","explanation_ar":"البلاغة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A school assembly: Adam at a podium giving a speech titled \"Waste Less, Share More\"; a screen behind him showing a photo of food being thrown away and a statistic; students and teachers listening attentively. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A school assembly: Adam at a podium giving a speech titled \"Waste Less, Share More\"; a screen behind him showing a photo of food being thrown away and a statistic; students and teachers listening attentively"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “The Power of Persuasion”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “The Power of Persuasion”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L11-U4-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin the Review Wheel”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin the Review Wheel"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS5-T1","script":"As a volunteer at our local food bank for the past two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes. Last winter, I met a mother who had not eaten for two days so that her children could. Meanwhile, millions of tons of food are thrown away every year around the world. Can we really accept this situation? I don't think so. Some people argue that one family's waste makes no difference; however, this argument ignores the power of small actions. In other words, if every family saves a little, the total will be enormous. What is more, food banks in our city make it easy to donate, and many families already do so. Let us waste less and share more, starting today.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U4-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Speech That Moves People: Language in Use”. Let’s learn substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and appeals, clear pronoun reference; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Persuasion challenge: prepare a 2-minute speech on a cause you care about (food waste, reading, kindness online, water saving). Include one example each of ethos, pathos and logos, one rhetorical question, two discourse markers, one substitution (so / not / do so) and one rebuttal of a counterargument. The audience identifies the three appeals. Today you use all of Unit 4: substitution and ellipsis, discourse markers and cohesion, rhetoric vocabulary and clear pronoun reference. A persuasive speech opens with credibility or a striking story, develops reasons with evidence, answers a counterargument (a rebuttal), and ends with a call to action. Discourse markers guide listeners; substitution keeps the speech flowing; clear pronouns prevent confusion. Here are some examples. As a volunteer for two years, I have seen hunger with my own eyes. (ethos) I met a mother who had not eaten for two days. (pathos) Millions of tons of food are thrown away every year. (logos) Be careful! Some learners say: “Can we accept this? I don't think it.” That’s not right. We say: “Can we accept this situation? I don't think so.” Summary noun + so. Quick check: what is wrong with “The food bank needs volunteers for their new program.”? … Think! … It should be “The food bank needs volunteers for its new program.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can deliver a persuasive speech with all three appeals.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"\"Is the evidence reliable?\" \"I believe ___.\"","options":["yes","that","so","it"],"answer":"so","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"\"Will the speaker be late?\" \"I hope ___.\"","options":["not","no","not so","don't"],"answer":"not","explanation":"so / not after think, hope, be afraid.","explanation_ar":"so / not."},{"q":"Is the claim supported by data? ___, we should take it seriously.","options":["If not","Not if","If so","So if"],"answer":"If so","explanation":"if so / if not.","explanation_ar":"if so / if not."},{"q":"The manager promised to answer every complaint, and she ___ within a week.","options":["did it so","made so","so did","did so"],"answer":"did so","explanation":"do so (formal).","explanation_ar":"do so."},{"q":"Some influencers reveal paid partnerships; others ___.","options":["don't","don't reveal it so","not","aren't"],"answer":"don't","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"Critics argued that the new advertising rules would be impossible to enforce, but the government insisted that they ______ and promised strict penalties for companies that broke them.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["could","could be","could be so","could do"],"answer":"could be","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"The ad uses frightening images; ___, it tries to scare people into buying.","options":["namely","for instance","anyway","in other words"],"answer":"in other words","explanation":"Reformulating (in other words, namely).","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة."},{"q":"Sara and Noura gave speeches; ___ used humor, while the latter used statistics.","options":["the former","former","the latter","the first one"],"answer":"the former","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."},{"q":"Don't judge her too quickly; ___, she has only just started.","options":["to begin with","finally","after all","namely"],"answer":"after all","explanation":"Contrast, concession, after all.","explanation_ar":"المقابلة والتنازل."},{"q":"In a formal essay, which marker is best for adding a strong point?","options":["What is more,","Well,","Anyway,","Mind you,"],"answer":"What is more,","explanation":"Adding and emphasizing.","explanation_ar":"الإضافة والتوكيد."},{"q":"Some online stores show fake countdown timers to make customers buy quickly. ______ is considered unfair by consumer protection groups.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["This","This sales tactic","Which","It"],"answer":"This sales tactic","explanation":"Reference: this + noun, the former / the latter.","explanation_ar":"الإحالة."},{"q":"\"As a nurse with twenty years' experience…\" This sentence appeals mainly to ___.","options":["pathos","logos","popularity","ethos"],"answer":"ethos","explanation":"Ethos, pathos or logos.","explanation_ar":"الاستمالات."},{"q":"The professor ___ the theory by showing that its data were incorrect.","options":["swayed","refuted","refused","conceded"],"answer":"refuted","explanation":"Rhetoric word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"\"Everyone is buying this phone, so it must be the best.\" This fallacy is ___.","options":["an appeal to popularity","logos","attacking the person","a slippery slope"],"answer":"an appeal to popularity","explanation":"Name the fallacy.","explanation_ar":"المغالطة."},{"q":"Although she remained convinced that the plan was risky, the councillor ______ that it would create jobs, a point her opponents had stressed throughout the debate.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["refuted","asserted falsely","conceded","swayed"],"answer":"conceded","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The documentary presented ___ evidence that plastic is harming sea turtles.","options":["eloquent","compelling","cohesive","emotive"],"answer":"compelling","explanation":"Rhetoric collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"","options":["When he met his coach, he explained his plan.","When Rakan met his coach, Rakan explained his plan.","When Rakan met his coach, he explained his plan.","When Rakan met him, he explained his plan."],"answer":"When Rakan met his coach, Rakan explained his plan.","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun (two possible antecedents).","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"","options":["In the article, they claim that sugar is addictive.","It claims in it that sugar is addictive.","The article claims that sugar is addictive.","In the article, it claims that sugar is addictive."],"answer":"The article claims that sugar is addictive.","explanation":"Pronoun with no antecedent.","explanation_ar":"ضمير بلا مرجع."},{"q":"The debate society changed ______ rules after several members complained that speakers were being interrupted too often.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["it's","their","they're","its"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"Supporters claimed that the advertisement was harmless. ______ critics showed that it had misled thousands of parents about the product's benefits.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Therefore,","For instance,","Similarly,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A hasty generalization draws a broad conclusion from too few examples.\n• An example: \"My two cousins dislike math, so all teenagers dislike math.\"\n• Such reasoning ignores the experiences of most people.\n• Good arguments use large, representative samples.\n\nThe student wants to explain why a hasty generalization is a weak argument. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["A hasty generalization is a type of reasoning.","Some teenagers dislike math.","Good arguments are important.","A hasty generalization is weak because it draws a broad conclusion from too few examples, ignoring the experiences of most people."],"answer":"A hasty generalization is weak because it draws a broad conclusion from too few examples, ignoring the experiences of most people.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the committee announced their decision, which upset them.\"","options":["The committee announced their decision, a choice that upset many members.","The committee announced its decision, a choice that upset many members.","The committee announced its decision, which upset them.","the committee announced its decision, a choice that upset many members."],"answer":"The committee announced its decision, a choice that upset many members.","explanation":"Collective noun or organization + its.","explanation_ar":"الاسم الجماعي."},{"q":"\"Some voters were persuaded; others weren't.\" What has been left out after \"weren't\"?","options":["some","voters","were","persuaded"],"answer":"persuaded","explanation":"Ellipsis after auxiliaries and modals.","explanation_ar":"الحذف بعد المساعد."},{"q":"Which sentence contains a false choice?","options":["Some students may get sick, so we should be careful.","If many students are sick, we may cancel the trip.","Either we cancel the trip or everyone will get sick.","We will discuss the trip tomorrow."],"answer":"Either we cancel the trip or everyone will get sick.","explanation":"Name the fallacy.","explanation_ar":"المغالطة."}],"theme_ar":"قوة الإقناع","essential_question_ar":"ما الذي يجعل الحجة مقنعة، وكيف نقاوم التلاعب؟","can_do":"I can avoid repetition with substitution and ellipsis (so, not, do so, one, others don't), connect ideas with discourse markers and cohesive devices, use the vocabulary of rhetoric and argument, and make pronoun references clear.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أتجنب التكرار بالاستبدال والحذف (so / not / do so / one / others don't)، وأربط الأفكار بأدوات الخطاب والتماسك، وأستخدم مفردات البلاغة والحجاج، وأجعل الإحالة بالضمائر واضحة."});
